{"assertion":{"kind":"theoretical","text":"Erdős Problem #456: declared status 'open'. Formalized: yes. Let $p_n$ be the smallest prime $\\equiv 1\\pmod{n}$ and let $m_n$ be the smallest integer such that $n\\mid \\phi(m_n)$.\n\nIs it true that $m_n<p_n$ for almost all $n$? Does $p_n/m_n\\to \\infty$ for almost all $n$? Are there infinitely many primes $p$ such that $p-1$ is the only $n$ for which $m_n=p$? Current best: Linnik's theorem implies that $p_n\\leq n^{O(1)}$. It is trivial that $m_n\\leq p_n$ always. van Doorn in the comments has noted that if $n=2^{2k+1}$ then $m_n\\leq 2n$ and $p_n\\geq 2n+1$. Prize: no. Tags: number theory."},"claim_id":"vcl_a72d4bd2ba6edc13b88d288da1a34c046c079168f0dd36f815ff869e108c6fad","conditions":[],"created_at":"2026-06-16T00:00:00Z","evidence":[],"extensions":{"vela.legacy-finding.v1":{"annotations":[],"attachments":[],"confidence":{"basis":"external catalogue record","extraction_confidence":1.0,"kind":"frontier_epistemic","method":"llm_initial","score":0.5},"flags":{"contested":false,"declining":false,"gap":false,"gravity_well":false,"negative_space":false,"retracted":false},"legacy_conditions":{"duration":null,"text":""},"legacy_evidence":{"evidence_spans":[],"method":"erdos_deep","model_system":"n/a","replicated":false,"replication_count":null,"type":"theoretical"},"legacy_links":[],"legacy_provenance":{"authors":[],"doi":null,"extraction":{"extracted_at":"","extractor_version":"","method":"database_import","model":null,"model_version":null},"review":null,"source_type":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:456","year":null},"previous_version":null,"source_schema":"https://vela.science/schema/finding-bundle/v0.10.0","source_version":1,"updated":null}},"imported_from":{"era":"finding_v0_10","object_id":"vf_437f692d0e63f437","object_root":"sha256:95ab7176147718a5cc84ccf27e1b6704502431c113f0df7797a4485a418844ac","predecessor_commit":"c236b4fdedb25e0d726e24e2780d50962e4844f7"},"provenance":[{"kind":"database_record","title":"erdo